#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222306} :activity/id #uuid "4bdf9815-fa54-50ce-b3f5-ad6d001dd7d2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050054 :topic/id #uuid "29ce72ae-dbea-5d83-a81d-90545461cdec" :topic/math-academy-id 6375} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222473 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll learn how to compute the **matrix exponential**.\n\nRecall that the scalar exponential function $e^t$ can be written as the power series \n\n$$\ne^t = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{t^k}{k!} = 1 + t + \\dfrac{t^2}{2} + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!} + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nNow, let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix. By definition, the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ is given by the analogous series\n\n$$\ne^{At} = \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!} = I + tA + \\dfrac{t^2}{2}A^2 + \\cdots + \\dfrac{t^k}{k!}A^k + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nThere are several approaches for computing matrix exponentials:\n\n- The most direct approach is to use the definition above, but this is typically impractical.\n\n- Another method, often covered in linear algebra courses, is to use the Jordan canonical form of $A$. This works, but it can also be tedious, especially for larger matrices.\n\nHere, we will introduce a different method.\n\nBy the Cayley–Hamilton theorem, every power $A^n, A^{n+1}, \\ldots$ can be rewritten as a linear combination of \n\n$$\nI, \\: A, \\: A^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: A^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, $e^{At}$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1}A^{n-1} t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_0, \\alpha_1, \\ldots, \\alpha_{n-1}$ are functions of $t$ and $I$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix. \n\nNow, we define the polynomial \n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0.\n$$\n\nThen we have the following key fact:\n\n> For any eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of $At$, we have $r(\\lambda) = e^{\\lambda}.$\n\nLet’s now see how this method works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "d78b3664-4117-56fc-bf30-5ca3acb28377" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14270 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f932ab79-9fa6-5a87-aa94-755b1685c4e3" :step/math-academy-id 588472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222482 :tutorial/content "Let's find $e^{At}$ for $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic polynomial $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we can find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7.\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2t, \\qquad\n\\lambda_2=7t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t) &={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(7t) &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t} \\\\ 7t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5t\\alpha_1 = e^{7t} - e^{2t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_1 = \\dfrac{1}{5t}\\bigl(e^{7t}-e^{2t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $7$ and subtracting twice the second one, we get\n\n$$\n5\\alpha_0 = 7e^{2t} - 2e^{7t}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\alpha_0 = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\bigl(7e^{2t}-2e^{7t}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the entries of $e^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((2{e}^{7t}-2{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ -7{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) \\\\ 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =7(\\frac{1}{5t}({e}^{7t}-{e}^{2t}))t+\\frac{1}{5}(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((7{e}^{7t}-7{e}^{2t})+(7{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{7t})) \\\\  & ={e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 7({e}^{2t}-{e}^{7t}) & 5{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e5aa695-b237-54d4-9836-20514d70dc9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14389 :tutorial/title "2x2 Matrices With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "3c91134a-154b-5692-a2cc-fdc45c81af7e" :step/math-academy-id 588473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221150 :question/id #uuid "35a158c3-37b6-5123-ba90-1fd05218ce43" :question/math-academy-id "e-21271"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee186e0-579f-5104-b0e1-50540abc0bdc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308645}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ebe4cfdc-9e1a-577d-ad38-9d117b4cf820" :step/math-academy-id 588474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221151 :question/id #uuid "18064c11-63d3-599a-b504-7b589f46c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370129}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ea0b2d60-3227-5e25-b6a9-171c0dc66319" :step/math-academy-id 588478 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222481 :tutorial/content "Recall that the matrix exponential $e^{At}$ can be written as\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\alpha_{n-1} A^{n-1}t^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2} A^{n-2}t^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1 At+\\alpha_0 I.\n$$\n\nIf we define the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda)=\\alpha_{n-1}\\lambda^{n-1}+\\alpha_{n-2}\\lambda^{n-2}+\\cdots+\\alpha_1\\lambda+\\alpha_0,\n$$\n\nthen for any eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ of $At,$ we have\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda_i) = e^{\\lambda_i}.\n$$\n\nHowever, for *repeated eigenvalues*, we have the following additional fact:\n\n> If $\\lambda_i$ is an eigenvalue of multiplicity $k$ with $k>1,$ then \n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{\\lambda }^{2}}r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}},\\,…,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{k-1}}{\\text{d}{\\lambda }^{k-1}}r(\\lambda ){|}_{\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{i}}={e}^{{\\lambda }_{i}}\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n\nLet's apply these facts to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d7b15b25-b73f-5d5f-aa02-e4c1a5e9804e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14388 :tutorial/title "2x2 Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "855a741b-2bbe-5e04-b892-8d1d9c7d0e65" :step/math-academy-id 588481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221410 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221152 :question/id #uuid "40a6318a-a1c9-55f3-afdb-02b43891d7b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "de5a7d5c-0c01-5247-8ba6-a69a863c9cbb" :step/math-academy-id 588482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222312}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222312 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222505 :tutorial/content "Again, let $A$ be a constant $n\\times n$ matrix. The matrix exponential satisfies\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\bigl(e^{At}\\bigr)=A\\,e^{At}.\n$$\n\nIndeed, recall that\n\n$$\ne^{At}=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(At)^k}{k!}\n=\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{A^k t^k}{k!}.\n$$\n\nTo prove the formula, we differentiate the series above term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}\\cdot k{t}^{k-1}}{k!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{k-1}{t}^{k-1}}{(k-1)!} \\\\  & =A{\\sum }_{j=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{A}^{j}{t}^{j}}{j!} \\\\  & =A\\,{e}^{At}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "739548ab-310c-5de3-a426-1fcaa3282478" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14526 :tutorial/title "Derivatives of Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "fc10fbf4-2be0-5b3f-ad2e-0505b175d5a6" :step/math-academy-id 588485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222313}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222313 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221411 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221194 :question/id #uuid "c0213c76-94e8-5ab3-a260-8a78a205589a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370321}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "df4956ab-0323-513e-ab21-66734987f4d0" :step/math-academy-id 588486 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222506 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials have other properties. We list a few of them:\n\n- *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n$$\ne^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n$$\n\n- *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n(e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n$$\n\n- *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n$$\ne^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n$$\n\n- *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n$$\ne^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fcc3801-5d58-5c8e-b8c8-7be65b34f71a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14527 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "24b8ab46-a4b1-5358-abbd-db086f102dbf" :step/math-academy-id 588489}] :activity/title "Matrix Exponentials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221467}]}
